 |ShortSummary= Bourdain visits Shanghai, witnessing firsthand the effects of China's booming economy on the vast nation. He explores aspects of Chinese history and culture that still resonate today, and seeks out the best street food. Bourdain also crashes a wedding.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=3
 |EpisodeNumber=29
 |Title = The Bronx
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|10|05}}
 |ShortSummary=Anthony Bourdain explores the rich cultural diversity and unique variety of cuisines the Bronx has to offer. From the Bronx's role in the birth of hip-hop to traditional Jamaican tonics to deep fried pig parts, Bourdain unearths the energy, vibe and rhythm of this often overlooked borough of [[New York City]].
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=4
 |EpisodeNumber=30
 |Title = Paraguay
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|10|12}}
 |ShortSummary=A South American country of 6&nbsp;million, much of the oppressively hot landlocked nation of Paraguay is jungle terrain or desert known as "[[Gran Chaco|the Chaco]]." It also holds a Bourdain family mystery.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=5
 |EpisodeNumber=31
 |Title = Vietnam
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|10|19}}
 |ShortSummary=Bourdain visits the former Vietnamese Imperial capital of [[Huế]] in [[Central Vietnam]], the nation's spiritual, cultural and culinary capital, where he tries local specialties such as [[Bún bò Huế]], [[Cơm hến]] (clams with rice topped with clam broth and [[pork rinds]]), [[Bánh bèo]] and [[:vi:Bánh bột lọc|Bánh bột lọc]] ([[cassava]] flour cakes topped with pan-fried shrimp, pork belly & green onions) at street-side vendors and restaurants. He visits [[Đông Ba Market]], a local artist's home sampling Vietnamese imperial court cuisine, a local fishing village, and the communist [[Vịnh Mốc tunnels]] north of the former [[Vietnamese Demilitarized Zone|DMZ]]. Anthony revisits the 1968 [[Tet Offensive]], including the [[Battle of Huế]] and the [[Huế Massacre]], where 3000 civilians were massacred by the [[Viet Cong]].
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=6
 |EpisodeNumber=32
 |Title = Tanzania
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|10|26}}
 |ShortSummary=Leaving behind the Africa we've come to know in the news — full of corruption, poverty and conflict — Bourdain embraces the wild, untamed continent of mystery, adventure and exoticism during a visit to Tanzania.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=7
 |EpisodeNumber=33
 |Title = Iran
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|11|02}}
 |ShortSummary=Bourdain shines a light on how Iranians are existing within an oppressive government system. He visits [[Isfahan]] and [[Tehran]] and stops at two private homes for traditional Persian cooking. The episode ends with a nightlife scene in Northern Tehran. This episode features [[Jason Rezaian]] and his wife and journalist Yeganeh Salehi before they were taken into custody by Iranian authorities in 2014.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=8
 |EpisodeNumber=34
 |Title = Massachusetts
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|11|09}}
 |ShortSummary=Bourdain visits [[Provincetown]] on [[Cape Cod]] and reminisces about the time he spent there as a young adult. He then travels to [[Franklin County, Massachusetts|Franklin County]] in western Massachusetts for an exploration of the heroin epidemic in the area.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=9
 |EpisodeNumber=35
 |Title = Jamaica
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|11|16}}
 |ShortSummary=Bourdain visits the coastal city of [[Port Antonio]] and meets with billionaire businessman [[Michael Lee-Chin]] and his business partner, [[Jon Baker (producer)|Jon Baker]], who are attempting to increase tourism to the area. He then visits [[Goldeneye (estate)|Goldeneye]], [[James Bond]] novelist [[Ian Fleming]]'s estate, and nearby [[James Bond Beach]], and has dinner with Goldeneye's current owner, legendary music producer [[Chris Blackwell]]. He also meets with various locals, and enjoys local dishes such as [[jerk chicken]], [[curry goat]] and [[ackee and saltfish]], while discussing the tensions between rich and poor, and tourists and locals, on the island. Parts of the episode have a soundtrack reminiscent of [[James Bond films]] of the 1960s.
 |LineColor=FDEE00
}}
}}

