 |EpisodeNumber2=5
 |EpisodeNumber=40
 |Title = Madagascar
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|05|17}}
 |ShortSummary= With acclaimed film director [[Darren Aronofsky]] as his travel companion, Tony explores this island nation off the southeastern coast of Africa. Starting the journey in the chaotic, crowded capital city [[Antananarivo|Tana]], they sample the cuisine of legendary [[Malagasy people|Malagasy]] chef Mariette Andrianjaka.
 |LineColor=98FF98
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=6
 |EpisodeNumber=41
 |Title = New Jersey
 |RTitle=<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nj.com/camden/index.ssf/2015/06/camdens_donkeys_place_swamped_after_getting_recogn.html |title=Anthony Bourdain's visit to Donkey's Place in Camden brings in extra large lunch crowd |website=The Star-Ledger |date=June 2, 2015 |access-date=October 28, 2017}}</ref>
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|05|31}}
 |ShortSummary= Bourdain travels to his childhood home state with his brother Chris and rekindles memories at classic roadside joint Hiram's, known for its fried hotdogs, and Barnegat Light in [[Ocean County, New Jersey|Ocean County]]. He heads further down the shore to [[Atlantic City]] and stops by the [[Knife and Fork Inn|Knife and Fork]], [[Dock’s Oyster House|Docks Oyster House]] with local reporter Brian Donohue, the Baltimore Grill to see husband and wife comedians Rich Vos and Bonnie McFarlane, and tours [[Asbury Park, New Jersey|Asbury Park]] with none other than famed musician [[Southside Johnny]]. Trips to [[Camden, New Jersey|Camden]] and the [[Pine Barrens (New Jersey)|Pine Barrens]] are included as well.
 |LineColor=98FF98
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=7
 |EpisodeNumber=42
 |Title = Budapest
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|06|07}}
 |ShortSummary= Drawing inspiration from iconic Hungarian born cinematographer [[Vilmos Zsigmond]], Bourdain explores the beauty, culture, history, architecture and food of Budapest. The culinary dishes sampled during his journey include [[goulash]], fisherman's soup, blood sausage, stuffed cabbage and, during a stop at restaurant Pleh Csarda, a golden brown pancake heaped with chicken liver and bone marrow, fried [[schnitzel]] and venison stew.
 |LineColor=98FF98
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=8
 |EpisodeNumber=43
 |Title = Hawaii
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|06|14}}
 |ShortSummary= Bourdain explores the other Hawaii, the one that the 8&nbsp;million tourists don't see when they descend on the islands every year. Tony meets with travel writer and novelist [[Paul Theroux]], Chef Andrew Le of Pig and the Lady in [[Honolulu]], talent manager [[Shep Gordon]] and communes with residents of [[Molokai]] for a meal at an ancient oceanside fish pond.
 |LineColor=98FF98
}}
{{Episode list
 |EpisodeNumber2=9
 |EpisodeNumber=44
 |Title = Beirut
 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|06|21}}
 |ShortSummary= The season five finale takes the host back to Beirut. Nicknamed "The Paris of the Middle East," its nightlife is infamous, the population beautiful, and its cuisine legendary. During his travels, Bourdain meets up with freestyle artist Double A The Preecherman in the [[Mar Mikhael]] neighborhood, has a classic Lebanese meal with writer, publisher, activist Joumana Haddad, and visits a Syrian community in southern Beirut with CNN correspondent [[Nick Paton Walsh]].
 |LineColor=98FF98
}}
